[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The eBGP subsystem (FRR bgpd) between router1 and router2 is healthy. Both routers show the BGP session Established with zero drops: “Connections established 1; dropped 0” and steadily increasing Up/Down timers (router1: Up/Down ~00:03:11; router2: ~00:03:11). Each side advertises/receives exactly one IPv4 Unicast prefix, and both routers’ kernel routing tables contain the learned eBGP route to the opposite LAN (router1: 200.1.1.0/24 via 193.10.11.2; router2: 195.11.14.0/24 via 193.10.11.1). Host reachability tests from pc1 to pc2 and back show 0% loss with sub‑millisecond RTT. The inter-router link (eth0) and access links (eth1) report no RX/TX errors or drops.

Mechanism: If the eBGP session were resetting “every few minutes,” we would see route withdrawals, kernel route flapping, and BGP “dropped” counters/log resets. Instead, FRR logs show regular 60s keepalives and no resets after startup. The solitary “nexthop_set failed … resetting connection” message appears once at FRR initialization and is not recurring.

Localization: No faults detected on router1:eth0/eth1, router2:eth0/eth1, or bgpd processes.

Root cause: No network-side fault present; the suspected periodic BGP resets are not occurring (initial transient during service start only).

Corrective fix: Do not bounce either router. No restart is required. If users still report intermittency, investigate host/application layers or external factors; on the network, monitoring shows stable BGP and clean interfaces. === END ===